  abstract = {Many feedforward neural network architectures (such as backpropagation) have the property that the weights of two units on a hidden layer can be interchanged without altering the input/output function of the network, given appropriate rearrangements of the weights on the next layer. Networks of this type are termed permutable. The existence of this property implies that the weight space of such a network has symmetries which could reduce the difficulty of finding a suitable set of weights for a particular application. In this paper it is shown that the set of invertible linear weight transformations that leave the input/output transfer function of the entire network unchanged form a subgroup of the group of invertible linear mappings from the weight space to itself. Further, it is shown that there exists a cone in weight space in which each weight equivalence class generated by this subgroup has an element. Thus, in searching for a suitable set of weights, only weights in this cone need to be considered. The fraction of weight space occupied by the cone is much larger than the inverse of the order of the subgroup. It is shown that much smaller weight search sets can be constructed.},
